        "Stibel hired Basie as permanent leader in May 1935, after the death of Bennie Moten had broken up the city’s leading band [1][3]. With Lester Young, Jo Jones, Walter Page and Jimmy Rushing, the small Reno band played head arrangements built from riffs. By August 1935 it was broadcast nightly over W9XBY, an experimental high-fidelity station [1][4]. In early 1936 the producer John Hammond heard the broadcasts on his car radio and soon came to Kansas City. By year’s end Basie had left for Chicago and New York with a larger band [1][3]. One story has Charlie Parker gaining his “Yardbird” nickname there, but it is unverified [2].",
        "The Reno lived on Pendergast’s protection. When the federal investigation of the machine began in 1938, that protection ended. State raids followed in spring 1939, and a court enjoined the club as a “disorderly house” [1]. Stibel was fined and lost his license, and the building was later demolished [1].",
