        "Gus Greenlee ran the Hill District’s numbers lottery, which by one account took in $20,000–25,000 a day. He used the money to build a Black-owned entertainment and sports empire: the Pittsburgh Crawfords, Negro League champions in 1935–36; Greenlee Field (1933); and the Crawford Grill [1]. The first Grill, in the Leader House at 1401 Wylie Avenue, is usually dated to 1933, though SABR gives 1931. It burned in 1951, and its site was later cleared for the Civic Arena [1][2].",
        "In 1943 (some sources say 1945) Greenlee and Joseph Robinson opened a second Crawford Grill farther up Wylie Avenue at Elmore Street. Greenlee sold it to Joe and Bill Robinson in 1950 [2][3]. Its stage drew touring stars including Art Blakey, Charles Mingus, Max Roach, Miles Davis, John Coltrane, Bill Evans, Clifford Brown and Thelonious Monk. It was also where young Pittsburghers such as George Benson and Stanley Turrentine started out [2][3]. It survived the 1968 riots that destroyed much of the Hill’s business district [4]. The second Grill closed in 2003 [2]. A state historical marker went up in 2001, and the building was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2020. An investment group bought it in 2010, but it has not reopened [2][3].",
